en-point Democratic partisan lean anchors the race", "Open race with a term-limited Republican president removes incumbency effects", "No state polls or expert ratings yet, so fundamentals carry the forecast", "Uncertain 2028 national environment as both parties pick successors"], "rationale": "Washington's long Democratic presidential record and ten-point partisan lean give the Democratic nominee a clear edge in an open race, and with no state polls or expert ratings available the forecast rests on those fundamentals.", "watch": "Early polling once the parties settle on nominees, or a marked shift in the national environment, could move the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Washington has voted Democratic in every presidential election since 1988, and its roughly ten-point Democratic lean sets a clear starting point for 2028. With a term-limited Republican president leaving office, both parties face open nominating contests, and no candidate has yet built a Washington-specific campaign. No public polls of the state and no expert ratings are available this far out.\n\nThe forecast therefore rests on fundamentals: the state's lean, the absence of incumbency effects, and a neutral national environment. Headlines about the 2028 field, including Democratic debates over moving left and Republican jockeying to succeed the term-limited president, suggest the national tone could matter, but nothing yet points to a Washington-specific shift. The state remains clearly favorable ground for the Democratic nominee, though the margin could move as actual candidates emerge and polling begins.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.873, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8189, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "20